  q(INF,'Which CDC guidelines treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious for every patient?','Standard precautions',['Airborne precautions','Reverse isolation','Contact precautions'],'B','Standard precautions apply to all patients regardless of diagnosis.',BLESI),
  q(SPEC,'How should a patient collect a sputum specimen?','Cough deeply in the morning and spit into a sterile container',['Spit saliva from the mouth into a cup','Collect it after brushing with mouthwash','Collect it after breakfast'],'C','Deep sputum from the lungs is needed; saliva contaminates the sample.',BLESI),
  q(SPEC,'Before collecting a wound culture, what should be done?','Clean away old drainage, then swab the wound tissue',['Apply antibiotic ointment first so the swab picks up only the medicine','Swab the outer skin only','Soak the wound in alcohol'],'D','Cleaning away surface debris improves the accuracy of the culture.',BLESI),
  q(SPEC,'Which stool test looks for parasites and their eggs?','Ova and parasites (O&P)',['Occult blood test','Fecal fat test','Fecal DNA test'],'A','O&P tests may require several specimens collected on different days.',BLESI),
  q(SPEC,'In a capillary puncture, why is the first drop of blood wiped away?','It may contain tissue fluid and alcohol that change results',['It is always too thick to test and will clog the testing device or tube','It contains too many platelets','It is needed to clean the finger'],'B','Wiping the first drop gives a more accurate sample; then collect without squeezing hard.',BLESI),
  q(SPEC,'Which practice can cause hemolysis of a blood specimen?','Using a needle that is too small or pulling hard on a syringe',['Mixing tubes gently by inversion','Letting the alcohol dry before puncture','Using the correct tube order'],'C','Hemolysis can falsely raise potassium and other test results.',BLESI),
  q(DX,'On an ECG, what does the T wave represent?','Ventricular repolarization',['Atrial depolarization','Ventricular depolarization','Atrial repolarization'],'D','The P wave is atrial depolarization and the QRS is ventricular depolarization.',BLESI),
  q(DX,'Which device records a patient’s heart rhythm continuously for 24 to 48 hours during normal activity?','A Holter monitor',['A spirometer','A peak flow meter','A pulse oximeter'],'A','Patients keep a diary of activities and symptoms while wearing it.',BLESI),
  q(DX,'Which device measures how fast a patient can blow air out, often used to monitor asthma at home?','A peak flow meter',['A Holter monitor','An otoscope','A glucometer'],'B','Falling peak flow readings can warn of worsening asthma before symptoms become severe.',BLESI),
  q(DX,'When is a tuberculin skin test (PPD) read?','48 to 72 hours after it is given',['Immediately after injection','After 1 week','After 2 weeks'],'C','The area of induration, not redness, is measured in millimeters.',BLESI),
  q(DX,'Which chart is used to test near vision?','A Jaeger card',['A Snellen chart','Ishihara plates','An E chart at 20 feet'],'D','The Jaeger card is held about 14 inches from the eyes, about normal reading distance.',BLESI),
  q(CLIN,'At what angle is an intradermal injection given?','10 to 15 degrees',['45 degrees','90 degrees','30 to 45 degrees'],'A','Intradermal injections, such as TB tests, form a small wheal under the skin.',BLESI),
  q(CLIN,'Why is the Z-track method used for some intramuscular injections?','To keep irritating drugs from leaking into tissue',['To give the medicine faster so it reaches the bloodstream right away','To avoid using alcohol prep','To use a smaller needle'],'B','The skin is pulled to one side before injection and released after to seal the track.',BLESI),
  q(CLIN,'Which syringe is used for very small doses, such as a TB skin test?','A tuberculin syringe',['A 10-mL syringe','An insulin pen','A 60-mL syringe'],'C','Tuberculin syringes hold 1 mL and are marked in hundredths of a milliliter.',BLESI),
  q(CLIN,'Which device turns liquid medication into a mist that is inhaled over several minutes?','A nebulizer',['A spirometer','A pulse oximeter','A Holter monitor'],'D','Nebulizers are useful for young children and patients who cannot use inhalers well.',BLESI),
  q(CLIN,'What temperature should ear irrigation solution be?','About body temperature',['Ice cold, to reduce swelling','As hot as the patient can stand','Room temperature straight from a cold storeroom'],'A','Solution that is too hot or too cold can stimulate the inner ear and cause dizziness and nausea.',BLESI),
  q(EXAM,'Which position has the patient kneeling with the chest on the table and hips raised?','Knee-chest position',['Fowler’s position','Lithotomy position','Supine position'],'B','The knee-chest position is used for some rectal and prostate exams.',BLESI),
  q(EXAM,'Which exam method uses the eyes to observe the patient’s appearance, color and movement?','Inspection',['Palpation','Percussion','Auscultation'],'C','Inspection is usually the first step in a physical exam, followed by palpation and other methods.',BLESI),
  q(EXAM,'Which hearing test places a vibrating tuning fork on the top of the head?','The Weber test',['The Rinne test','The Romberg test','The Snellen test'],'D','The Weber test checks whether sound is heard equally in both ears.',BLESI),
  q(EXAM,'Which instrument is used to open the nostrils to examine the inside of the nose?','A nasal speculum',['An otoscope without a tip','A tuning fork','A tongue depressor'],'A','A lighted nasal speculum allows the provider to view the nasal passages.',BLESI),
  q(EXAM,'Measuring a patient’s height, weight or chest size is called what?','Mensuration',['Manipulation','Percussion','Auscultation'],'B','Mensuration is the process of measuring during an exam, such as height, weight or a wound’s size.',BLESI)
